+/* sierra-hd reference tooling for ScummVM's SCI engine.
+ *
+ * This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
+ * it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
+ * the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
+ * (at your option) any later version.
+ *
+ * The deterministic mode of milestone 4, on when SIERRA_HD_TRACE names a
+ * file:
+ *
+ * - Time is virtual. It advances only where the engine sleeps or waits
+ *   (SciEngine::sleep, the extra 10 ms delay in kGetEvent), never with the
+ *   host clock. Game ticks are 1/60 s of virtual time. The play time
+ *   (kGetTime(elapsed)) runs on the virtual clock too; restoring a saved
+ *   game sets it back to the save's play time, as on the host clock, and
+ *   takes the wait and screen-update times from the virtual clock (milestone
+ *   8). Virtual time itself runs on through a restore, as host time does.
+ * - The music timer runs on the virtual clock, once per base tempo of the
+ *   driver, on the main thread; the driver's own timer is not installed.
+ * - The "sci" random source is seeded with SIERRA_HD_SEED (default 0).
+ * - The day starts at Monday 1990-01-01, 12:00:00 and runs with the
+ *   virtual clock. The screen-update throttle (and its quit check) and the
+ *   text-edit cursor blink run on the virtual clock too.
+ * - Input comes only from SIERRA_HD_REPLAY (docs/formats/replay.md in
+ *   sierra-hd); host input other than quitting is ignored. The mouse
+ *   position is virtual too.
+ * - Every kernel call is written to the trace file, one line each:
+ *     <call> <tick> <name>(<arguments>) = <result>
+ *   with values as unsigned decimal numbers, or <segment>:<offset> in hex
+ *   for pointers.
+ * - SIERRA_HD_STOP_TICK and SIERRA_HD_STOP_CALLS end the game when the tick
+ *   or the number of kernel calls reaches them; should the game not stop,
+ *   the process ends a minute of virtual time or a million calls later.
+ * - With SIERRA_HD_SCREENS=<folder>, the screen the player sees is written
+ *   there as <tick>.ppm whenever the clock reaches the next multiple of
+ *   SIERRA_HD_SCREEN_EVERY ticks (default 60), once graphics are up.
+ * - kGetTime(elapsed) right after another kGetTime(elapsed), with no other
+ *   kernel call between them, lets 1 ms of virtual time pass first.
+ *
+ * Milestone 5 adds:
+ *
+ * - With SIERRA_HD_SCREENS, the priority and control maps the logic reads
+ *   (GfxScreen's, not what is shown) are written at the same ticks as
+ *   <tick>.priority.pgm and <tick>.control.pgm (binary PGM, 320 x 200,
+ *   values 0 to 15).
+ * - With SIERRA_HD_ACTORS=<file>, every kAnimate call writes its cast, one
+ *   line per entry in drawing order, right after its trace line:
+ *     <tick> <call> <object> <x> <y> <z> <priority> <view> <loop> <cel> <signal>
+ *   with the values GfxAnimate drew with (after fill() and
+ *   restoreAndDelete()), numbers in signed decimal and the signal as four
+ *   hex digits.
+ *
+ * Milestone 6a adds:
+ *
+ * - With SIERRA_HD_PARSER=<file>, every kParse and kSaid call writes one
+ *   line there, right after its trace line:
+ *     <tick> <call> parse "<input>" ok words=<words> tree=<tree> type=<n> claimed=<n>
+ *     <tick> <call> parse "<input>" syntax words=<words> type=<n> claimed=<n>
+ *     <tick> <call> parse "<input>" unknown word="<word>" type=<n> claimed=<n>
+ *     <tick> <call> parse "<input>" empty type=<n> claimed=<n>
+ *     <tick> <call> said <spec> <outcome> claimed=<n>
+ *   The input is quoted, with \", \\ and \xHH for other bytes outside 32 to
+ *   126. The words are those parseGNF got (after synonyms and pronouns),
+ *   separated by commas, each as <class>:<group> in hex (meanings separated
+ *   by |). The tree is _parserNodes from node 0: a branch as
+ *   (<left>,<right>), a leaf as its value in hex, a word as w<value>
+ *   followed by /<right> if it has one, a missing child as -. type and
+ *   claimed are the event's selectors when the call returns. The said spec
+ *   is its bytes in hex up to the 0xff that ends it (- if it cannot be
+ *   read); the outcome is null (no spec), invalid, skipped (no event or
+ *   already claimed), none, partial or full; claimed is the flag the call
+ *   leaves on the event (- without one).
+ *
+ * Milestone 6b adds:
+ *
+ * - With SIERRA_HD_LAYOUT=<file>, the window layout in 1x screen
+ *   coordinates: one line per window, text, control, menu and cursor
+ *   operation, as it happens, keyed by the tick and the number of the
+ *   kernel call running:
+ *     <tick> <call> <kind> <fields>
+ *   Rectangles are left,top,right,bottom (right and bottom exclusive),
+ *   strings are quoted as the parser's input. The kinds are listed in
+ *   docs/formats/replay-and-trace.md of sierra-hd, "Layout files".
+ *
+ * Milestone 7 adds:
+ *
+ * - The AdLib driver's own timer work (MidiDriver_AdLib::onTimer, which
+ *   ages the notes) runs right after each music timer call of the virtual
+ *   clock, on the main thread, and no longer on the OPL callback: the
+ *   driver chooses voices by age, so this is part of the determinism.
+ * - With SIERRA_HD_OPL=<file>, every OPL register write the AdLib driver
+ *   makes (MidiDriver_AdLib::setRegister) writes one line there:
+ *     <timer call> <register> <value>
+ *   The timer call is the number of music timer calls the virtual clock has
+ *   made (0 before the first); the register is three hex digits, 0xxx for
+ *   the left OPL2 (port 0x220) and 1xx for the right one (port 0x222); the
+ *   value two hex digits, as the driver writes it (before the dual OPL2
+ *   masks waveform and channel output bits).
+ * - With SIERRA_HD_MIDI=<file>, every message the SCI MIDI driver
+ *   (MidiPlayer_Midi, the MT-32 path) hands to the MIDI driver writes one
+ *   line there, before the emulator's queue:
+ *     <timer call> <byte> <byte> ...
+ *   The timer call as for SIERRA_HD_OPL; the bytes two hex digits each: a
+ *   channel message as its status and data bytes (two for a program change
+ *   or channel pressure, three otherwise), a SysEx message as passed to
+ *   MidiDriver::sysEx (for the driver's own: Roland header 41 10 16 12,
+ *   address, data and checksum, without F0 and F7).
+ */
+
